al order with the expected demand over the period. For item 0 that is about 736 (days 4–29); for item 1 it is about 692 (days 5–29). Both include the 1.046 average multiplier.\n\n| Candidate | Item 0 | Item 1 | Comment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 719 | 675 | Slightly below expected demand. A shortage costs 15–16 per unit, so running short is expensive. |\n| 4 | 771 (+35) | 730 (+38) | A moderate buffer. |\n| 6 | 838 (+102) | — | Item 0 is about 2.7 standard deviations above expected demand. Most of the excess would be left over at the end, worth about 8 each, so roughly 800 wasted. |\n| 3 | 737 | 695 | No buffer, and each 3-day cycle has more variation. |\n| 5 | 763 | 718 | A moderate buffer. Around day 26, item 1 arrives (110 units) for the demand spike of 62. Stock would be about 185 plus the buffer, so some units would likely be lost to the capacity limit. |\n| 2 | 839 | — | Too much item 0. Stock sits near the 188 limit (days 8 and 11), and the day 6 order would be cut for lack of cash. |\n\nCandidates 4 and 5 are the only two left with a reasonable buffer. Candidate 4's 2-day cycle keeps stock well below capacity and gives smaller swings in demand per cycle, so it holds up better if demand differs from the forecast."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3882, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3882}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-27T23:26:19.990Z", "request_id": "req_011CgTc4V7YJyCGDxJHV5qp7"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "conten